WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:09.348
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.277 --> 00:00:25.360
DREW BINHAIR: This is Drew Binhair, reporting live

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.460
from the Discretown Medical Clinic.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.180
The town is grappling with an outbreak of hypertangleosis, a condition which

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.970
makes hair tangled and gnarled.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.370
As you can see, the line is out the door.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.620
Let's go to Dr. Sarah Straighthair for the latest.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.270
Doctor, how is your clinic coping with this horrifying outbreak?

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.450
DR. STRAIGHTHAIR: Please, everybody, stay calm.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.660
Hypertangleosis is treatable and does not cause long-term health issues.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.600
We treat it at our clinic with a special conditioner which

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.670
usually cures the disease immediately.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.590
It's not always effective, though.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.340
We have limited capacity and can only treat 10 patients per day.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.977
We're working to address these issues.

00:01:01.977 --> 00:01:03.310
DREW BINHAIR: Thank you, Doctor.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.800
Good luck, and please keep us posted.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.280
We go now to Professor Joe Blitzstein from the Harvard statistics department,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.760
to tell us more about how to make sense of this disturbing situation.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.816
Professor, can you tell us how effective the treatment is?

00:01:15.816 --> 00:01:18.190
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Based on the limited data we have so far,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.400
we estimate that the treatment has a 60% chance of success

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.075
and a 40% chance of failure.

00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:24.700
DREW BINHAIR: Let me get this straight.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.110
So, each day, you expect that 6 out of the 10 treated patients will be cured?

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.560
JOE BLITZSTEIN: On average, 6 out of the 10 will be cured.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:42.320
But there could easily be more than six, or fewer than six on a particular day.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.870
We have to consider the whole distribution.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.704
DREW BINHAIR: You mean, who distributes the treatment to the clinic?

00:01:47.704 --> 00:01:49.411
JOE BLITZSTEIN: We should try to find out

00:01:49.411 --> 00:01:51.060
as much as we can about the treatment.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.040
But I meant distribution in the statistics sense.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.920
When we are looking at a quantity that can take on various possible values,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.900
the distribution tells us how likely any particular value or range of values is.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.240
Let's say we want to predict X, the number of patients

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.860
who will be cured tomorrow.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.050
The distribution of X specifies the answers

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.530
to questions like, what's the probability that X is 6?

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.920
Or what's the probability that X is between 4 and 8?

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.632
DREW BINHAIR: So what is the distribution of X?

00:02:18.632 --> 00:02:20.840
JOE BLITZSTEIN: We don't have enough data to be sure.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.420
A lot is unknown about this treatment.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:27.410
For example, is it more effective on young people than on older people?

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.560
Assuming that each patient has probability 0.6 of being treated

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.130
successfully, and that these patients are independent of each other,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:38.090
the distribution of X is called Binomial 10, comma, 0.6.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.460
Here's a picture of this Binomial distribution,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.800
showing how likely each possible value is.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.800
DREW BINHAIR: Hey, that looks just like my hair!

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.110
JOE BLITZSTEIN: You can see that 6 is the most likely value,

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.380
but that doesn't mean it's especially likely.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.210
Its probability is about 0.25.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.840
There's about a 90% chance, though, that X will be between 4 and 8.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.234
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:06.234 --> 00:03:07.400
DREW BINHAIR: Breaking news.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.240
This just in.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.070
Bimodality detected.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.550
Day 100 of our continuous coverage of the hypertangleosis crisis

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.170
has brought us a shocking statistical development.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.722
We go to Professor Blitzstein for more.

00:03:18.722 --> 00:03:21.680
JOE BLITZSTEIN: A few months ago, I suggested the Binomial distribution

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.460
as a plausible model for how many patients will get cured on a given day.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.120
Now, with much more data, it has become clear

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.840
that the Binomial model is inadequate.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.580
DREW BINHAIR: How so?

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.880
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Here's a histogram of how many patients were cured in a day

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.190
compared with the Binomial 10, comma, 0.6 distribution.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.790
As you can see, they look very different.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.000
We have a case of bimodality.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.270
There are two peaks in the distribution.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:53.150
I will be working closely with Dr. Straighthair to figure out why.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.107
DREW BINHAIR: So is everything you told us about Binomials useless?

00:03:56.107 --> 00:03:59.440
JOE BLITZSTEIN: There were many problems where the Binomial fits well, this just

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.000
isn't one of them.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.460
Even so, the Binomial gives us benchmarks

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.510
we can compare the real data to and serves as a building block

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.210
for various more complicated models.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:14.250
DREW BINHAIR: Please, Professor, keep us updated.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.982
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:15.982 --> 00:04:17.690
Dr. Straighthair and Professor Blitzstein

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.450
have been working tirelessly to unravel the mystery of bimodality.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.142
They're here now live to update us on the results.

00:04:24.142 --> 00:04:26.600
DR. STRAIGHTHAIR: Joe and I have been poring over the data,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.940
and have found an explanation we hope will not only

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.520
resolve the statistical anomaly, but also

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.510
let us improve the percentage of patients we can treat successfully.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.270
The conditioner comes from two different suppliers--

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.306
Normal distributors and Cauchy distributors.

00:04:40.306 --> 00:04:43.430
JOE BLITZSTEIN: We discovered that the conditioner from Normal distributors

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.270
is far more effective than the conditioner from Cauchy distributors.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:51.890
On each day, it was completely random which supplier was used.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.680
On days when the conditioner came from Normal distributors,

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.010
the success rate was 80%.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:01.880
But for Cauchy distributors, the success rate was only 40%.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:07.980
The overall distribution is a mixture of Binomials, not a Binomial.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.880
Here's the histogram of the data compared with the mixture distribution.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.656
As you can see, the mixture fits much better than the Binomial did.

00:05:16.656 --> 00:05:17.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.010
DREW BINHAIR: We're back, live, on day 110 of our coverage

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.100
of the hypertangleosis crisis.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.000
Cauchy distributors' stock price has plummeted in the last week,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.150
as it has become clear that their quality control is

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.690
far inferior to their competitor.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.880
In other news, Normal distributors has just

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.870
released a new formula of the conditioner

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.690
that patients can apply at home instead of at a clinic with limited capacity.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:46.529
Professor Blitzstein is here to help us predict the ramifications.

00:05:46.529 --> 00:05:48.570
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Hypertangleosis is rarer than you

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:53.740
may think, possibly because the media have been covering this 24 hours a day.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.940
It would be much better to obtain good data on how many cases there

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.950
are as a function of time.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.500
According to preliminary estimates, the probability

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.170
of coming down with hypertangleosis tomorrow

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:08.850
for someone whose hair is currently fine, is only 0.001.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:13.770
The number of people in Discretown with healthy hair is currently about 20,000.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.340
So we can expect about 20 new cases tomorrow.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.350
DREW BINHAIR: But what is the distribution?

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.150
JOE BLITZSTEIN: I'm glad you asked.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.670
Assuming the estimates I gave are correct,

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:27.690
and that people come down with hypertangleosis independently,

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:32.850
the number of new cases is Binomial 20,000, comma, 0.001.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.780
That's pretty unwieldy to work with, since calculations with it involve

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.570
extremely large numbers like 20,000 factorial,

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:45.120
which is about 10 to the 77,337th power.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.470
In general, the Binomial (n,p) distribution counts the number

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.300
of successes in n,p independent trials, each of which has probability, p,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.740
of success.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.370
If we have a Binomial (n,p) distribution, where p is small,

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:02.840
it turns out that we can approximate it well with a beautiful distribution

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.290
known as the Poisson.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.990
The Poisson has one parameter, usually called lambda.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.350
The probability of a Poisson of lambda--

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.660
random variable equaling k--

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:17.460
is e to the minus lambda, lambda to the k over k factorial

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.950
For any non-negative integer k.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:24.340
For the problem at hand, or should I say, at hair,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.280
we can use the Poisson of 20 as a good approximation

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.340
to the Binomial 20,000, comma, 0.001.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.510
DREW BINHAIR: That looks very pretty, like a bell-shaped curve.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.220
JOE BLITZSTEIN: That's another story.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.230
DREW BINHAIR: But tell us, Professor, how many of the new cases

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:39.665
do you predict will be cured?

00:07:39.665 --> 00:07:42.790
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Let's assume that the number of new cases is Poisson of 20,

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:47.410
and that they all try the new treatment with success probability 0.08.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.820
Then, the expected number of new cases treated successfully

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.160
is 0.8 times 20, which is 16.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.210
And the expected number treated unsuccessfully is 4.

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:01.480
The distribution of the number of successes is Poisson of 16.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.230
And the distribution of the number of failures is Poisson of 4.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.530
Surprisingly, these two quantities are independent,

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.110
with a Poisson number of new cases, knowing

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.045
the number of successes tells us nothing about the number of failures.

00:08:15.045 --> 00:08:17.920
DREW BINHAIR: Thank you, Professor, for explaining all of this to us.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.840
Any parting words?

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.690
JOE BLITZSTEIN: We need good data and good statistical thinking

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:25.740
in order to make good predictions.

